The evidence is consistent, so I'm writing up the findings. The findings are below. The JSON is in the schema you specified, with no markdown.

```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "ts-travel-plan-service",
      "fault_kind": "http_response_body_corrupted",
      "evidence": [
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT c.service_name AS caller, c.span_name AS caller_span, s.service_name AS callee, s.span_name AS callee_span, s.attr_http_response_status_code AS code, count(*) AS n FROM abnormal_traces c JOIN abnormal_traces s ON c.trace_id=s.trace_id AND c.span_id=s.parent_span_id WHERE c.service_name='ts-travel-plan-service' AND s.service_name='ts-seat-service' GROUP BY 1,2,3,4,5", "claim": "239 travel-plan calls to seat-service arrived as POST /error and returned 403, instead of seat left_tickets calls returning 200."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT p, span_name, count(*) AS n FROM (SELECT 'n' AS p, * FROM normal_traces UNION ALL SELECT 'a', * FROM abnormal_traces) WHERE service_name='ts-travel-plan-service' AND attr_span_kind='Client' AND span_name='POST' GROUP BY 1,2", "claim": "Normal period has 273 travel-plan calls to seat left_tickets; abnormal has none of those, only the rewritten /error calls."},
        {"kind": "log", "sql": "SELECT level, count(*) AS n, any_value(message) AS example FROM abnormal_logs WHERE service_name='ts-travel-plan-service' AND level='SEVERE' GROUP BY 1", "claim": "travel-plan logs 239 SEVERE 'Request processing failed' entries caused by 403 Forbidden from path /api/v2/."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "service": "ts-route-plan-service",
      "fault_kind": "application_method_slow",
      "evidence": [
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT span_name, attr_k8s_pod_name AS pod, count(*) AS n, round(median(duration)/1e6,1) AS med_ms, round(max(duration)/1e6,1) AS max_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='ts-route-plan-service' AND attr_span_kind='Internal' GROUP BY 1,2 ORDER BY 1", "claim": "Route-plan's own methods show tail self-time up to 8.3 s in one pod, while median stays near normal."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT c.span_name, round(c.duration/1e6,1) AS parent_ms, s.service_name AS child, round(s.duration/1e6,1) AS child_ms FROM abnormal_traces c JOIN abnormal_traces s ON c.trace_id=s.trace_id AND c.span_id=s.parent_span_id WHERE c.service_name='ts-route-plan-service' AND c.attr_span_kind='Internal' AND c.duration > 5e9 LIMIT 20", "claim": "In the slowest route-plan traces the parent method takes seconds while its downstream calls take only tens of milliseconds."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {"from": "ts-travel-plan-service", "to": "ts-ui-dashboard",
     "evidence": [{"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT c.service_name AS caller, s.service_name AS callee, s.attr_http_response_status_code AS code, count(*) AS n, round(median(c.duration)/1e6,1) AS caller_ms FROM abnormal_traces c JOIN abnormal_traces s ON c.trace_id=s.trace_id AND c.span_id=s.parent_span_id WHERE c.service_name='ts-ui-dashboard' AND s.service_name='ts-travel-plan-service' GROUP BY 1,2,3", "claim": "Dashboard calls to travel-plan return HTTP 500 for all 239 affected requests, with multi-second caller latency."}]},
    {"from": "ts-route-plan-service", "to": "ts-travel-plan-service",
     "evidence": [{"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT c.service_name AS caller, s.service_name AS callee, s.span_name, count(*) AS n, round(median(c.duration)/1e6,1) AS caller_ms, round(median(s.duration)/1e6,1) AS callee_ms FROM abnormal_traces c JOIN abnormal_traces s ON c.trace_id=s.trace_id AND c.span_id=s.parent_span_id WHERE c.service_name='ts-travel-plan-service' AND s.service_name='ts-route-plan-service' GROUP BY 1,2,3", "claim": "Travel-plan calls to route-plan minStopStations and quickestRoute show multi-second caller latency, matching route-plan's slow self-time."}]},
    {"from": "ts-travel-plan-service", "to": "ts-ui-dashboard",
     "evidence": [{"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT span_name, attr_http_response_status_code AS code, count(*) AS n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='ts-ui-dashboard' AND attr_http_response_status_code>=500 GROUP BY 1,2", "claim": "The dashboard's travelPlan endpoints return 500 responses to users, 11 of them in the abnormal period."}]}
  ]
}
```

**Summary of what I found**

- **Affected user path:** The degradation is on the dashboard's `/api/v1/travelplanservice/travelPlan/*` endpoints. Their median went from about 605 ms to 6.8 s, and 11 requests returned 500. Other dashboard endpoints look normal.
- **Fault 1, `ts-travel-plan-service`, `http_response_body_corrupted`:** Travel-plan's calls to seat-service were rewritten. Its 273 normal `seats/left_tickets` calls became 239 `POST /error` calls with path `/api/v2/`, which seat-service rejects with 403. Travel-plan then returns 500 to the dashboard. The rewrite changed the request path (the body/path category), not just the status code, because the seat-service spans and logs show a different path.
- **Fault 2, `ts-route-plan-service`, `application_method_slow`:** Route-plan's own methods (`getMinStopStations`, `getQuickestRoutes`) have tail self-time of several seconds, up to 8.3 s, in a single pod. Their downstream calls take only tens of milliseconds, and CPU stays well below the pod limit, so this is in-method slowness rather than CPU saturation or a database fault.
- **Propagation:** Route-plan's slowness reaches travel-plan, which reaches the dashboard. Travel-plan's 500s also reach the dashboard.
- **Ruled out:** Database spans are normal across services, there are no restarts, and the Hubble drop counters don't isolate a fault. Services that disappeared from the abnormal traces (cancel, consign-price) are a workload-mix effect, not a failure.
